WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
Good morning, Hank, it's Sunday, May 3rd, 2 days before your birthday.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:06.600
And as you can tell from the grey speckled wall behind me, I am in an airport.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.760
Fancy that.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.400
So Hank, today I'm gonna talk about swine flu - PANIC! But before I do that, I'm wanna talk about me. And you.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.400
Mostly me.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.360
Nerdfighters, if you wanted to go to the event in Melbourne, Australia, but you couldn't get a ticket,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.800
good news, there's a second event. Link in the sidebar.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.500
And also, I'm going to be in Sydney on June 3rd.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.340
Secondly, Hank, as you know, on Thursday night, my book, Paper Towns, won an Edgar Award.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.040
An award given out by the Mystery Writers of America to the best mystery novels of the year.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.820
Hank, this means that I now own a bust of Edgar Allen Poe. That's right, I own a bust.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.160
So Nerdfighters, I need your help. Please answer the following question for me in comments:

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.140
What can you do with a bust of Edgar Allen Poe?

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:46.780
[singing] What can you do with a Poe bust?

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.240
Oh, and Hank, about you, your birthday is on Tuesday, uh, I'm gonna getcha a little something.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.560
Hopefully nerdfighters, you'll make some videos and do some stuff as well.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.240
Please do not ask for my attention right now.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:04.480
So Hank, here's a little-known fact about me: I am somewhat terrified of new and strange diseases.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.320
I think it's because when I was in college, I had to fulfil a Science requirement, so I took a class called

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.880
'AIDS, Ebola, and Emerging Viruses', because it was purportedly easy.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:17.020
And it was kind of easy, but the downside was that I learned about AIDS, Ebola, and emerging viruses.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.200
Which are just not topics that hypochondriacs should be exposed to.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.600
Anyway, Hank, you could imagine my dismay when the news broke about H1N1 flu, or swine flu, or oinkbarf.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.500
I think oinkbarf is its technical name.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.780
Here was this flu in Mexico, travelling easily between people, killing 10% of its victims,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.380
most of whom were between 25 and 45.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.420
Hank, I find it alarming when people between 25 and 45 die of flu, first off, because that's the same thing that

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.120
happened during the pandemic of 1918, which killed more people than all of World War II.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.760
And secondly, because, Hank, I don't know if you've noticed this,

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.600
but it so happens that I am between 25 and 45!

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.160
So, of course, I was extremely alarmed.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.180
But then, since then it turns out that people may have been slightly overreacting.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.580
Uh, it appears for instance that oinkbarf does not kill 10% of its victims, but closer to 1%.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:05.280
And also it appears they kill not just those between 25 and 45, but, y'know, just people generally.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.940
Now Hank, I'm not gonna lie to you, I'm still a little bit terrified of oinkbarf.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.420
In fact, that's probably why I'm calling it oinkbarf.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:12.840
But what interests me is why we're all so afraid of it.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:18.040
I mean, obviously, there's the fact that if 10% of people who got it actually required hospitalisation,

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.320
we would, like, run out of hospital beds in short order and everyone would freak out.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.540
But my suspicion is that our fear of pandemic goes deeper than that.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.920
What pandemic forces us to think about is the fact that not only am I going to die, and you're going to die,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.460
but everyone's going to die.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:35.700
And in fact, there will come a time when there are no human beings left to remember that there were ever

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:36.480
human beings.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.020
Which is, I'm willing to acknowledge, a little bit alarming.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:43.320
Hank, F. Scott Fitzgerald famously wrote that the test of a first rate intelligence is the ability to hold

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.680
2 opposing ideas in the mind at the same time.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.720
In that same essay, he wrote," I must hold in balance the sense of the futility of effort,

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.820
and the sense of the necessity to struggle."

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.120
But in general, the way that we respond to the futility of effort is by, y'know, ignoring it.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.820
So I just don't think about the fact that someday that no one will read Shakespeare, let alone my books.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.560
Even though it is an Edgar Award winner.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.780
I don't think pandemics make us afraid of death, I think they make us afraid of oblivion.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.920
They force us to grapple with the futility of effort.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.860
Also, they make us barf, which isn't fun either.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.720
Hank, nerdfighters, wash your hands, cover your coughs,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.240
and find a way to hold in balance the futility of effort with the necessity to struggle.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.940
Hank, I, for one, am gonna keep struggling on. Ergo, you'll see me on Tuesday.

